    "text": "# Phishing and Credential Theft Threat Advisory\n\n## Overview\nPhishing attacks are malicious attempts to acquire sensitive information, such as usernames, passwords, credit card details, and bank account credentials, by masquerading as a trustworthy entity in electronic communication. In these campaigns, scammers typically clone the user interfaces of major banks, e-commerce giants, or streaming platforms and use deceptive domains. Advanced phishing now uses homograph attacks, typosquatting (e.g. g00gle.com), and excessive subdomains to hide the true threat origin.\n\n## Warning Signs\n* The domain name contains unusual dashes, numbers, or misspelled brand names.\n* The website forces immediate login to resolve an \"urgent\" account issue.\n* The URL ends with low-reputation or free Top-Level Domains (TLDs) like .xyz, .top, .cfd, or .win.\n* The page explicitly asks for OTPs, PINs, or secondary authentication tokens.\n\n## Prevention Guidance\n* Always manually type the official website address into your browser rather than clicking email or SMS links.\n* Enable Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) via authenticator apps (not SMS) on all crucial accounts.\n* Check the website's SSL certificate, but remember that scammers can easily obtain free SSL certificates.\n* Never download or execute attachments from unexpected sources that require immediate attention.\n",
