  {
    "text": "# UPI Payment Request Fraud Advisory\n\n## Overview\nUPI Payment Request Fraud, often known as the \"Reverse Request Scam\", is a widespread financial threat in digital payment systems. Scammers manipulate the UPI architecture by sending a \"collect request\" or \"debit request\" to a victim's VPA (Virtual Payment Address). They typically contact the victim claiming a refund, a cashback bonus, or an accidental transfer. The user is instructed to enter their UPI PIN under the false pretense that they are \"receiving\" money. However, entering a UPI PIN strictly authorizes a debit (sending money), resulting in immediate financial loss.\n\n## Warning Signs\n* Receiving a notification asking you to enter your UPI PIN to claim a refund, prize, or cashback.\n* The VPA handle of the requester contains suspicious words like \"refund\", \"claim\", \"mule\", or unusual numeric combinations.\n* The scammer creates an artificial sense of urgency, insisting the payment will fail if not verified immediately.\n* QR codes sent via WhatsApp that route directly to a high-value debit request on scanning.\n\n## Safe Practices\n* Entering a UPI PIN is strictly for SENDING money. You never need a PIN to RECEIVE money.\n* Do not scan QR codes received from unknown individuals on messaging apps.\n* If someone claims they sent money by mistake, instruct them to contact their bank's dispute resolution cell.\n* Decline and block any unverified \"collect requests\" in your UPI app immediately.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "upi_payment_scam.md",
      "filepath": "E:\\Scam Detection System\\backend\\knowledge_base\\upi_payment_scam.md"
